        "abstract": "In control and allied fields it is often necessary to determine experimentally the Transfer-Function or Input/Output ratio of a system either for obtaining data or to check a design. Although there are a number of techniques and instruments, usually expensive, to do this here a simple method using only a step-input signal and a modestly-priced personal computer obtaining results in realtime is described here. There are constraints as the computer must also be used for data capture. Although the final results are usually expressed as the ratio of two polynomials in the Laplace domain, as the results are stored in sampled form it is easier to solve for the denominator in the z-domain using the standard Least-Squares technique [1]. The result is then transferred to the S-domain. This is fairly well documented but the novelty of the method outlined here is the use of a second application of the Least-Squares technique to obtain the numerator coefficients. No technique can be accepted without testing. Hence small scale systems with and without noise, non-linearities and dead-times have been investigated. Special arrangements have had to be made to deal with systems with dead-times or to be approximated by a dead-time and exponential lags. The technique met the expectations within the limits of reasonable linearity and noise.",
